/*
  Psychiatric Interviewing Trainer — Study guide, Chapter 11: Psychotic Disorders: How to Sensitively Arrive at a Differential Diagnosis
  Written in our own words as a companion to Shea, Psychiatric Interviewing: The Art of Understanding (3rd ed.).
  Clinical exchanges are composed for teaching; they are not transcripts from the book. Book cases are summarized.
  Schema: see guide-ch1.js.
*/
window.TRAINER = window.TRAINER || { parts: {} };
window.TRAINER.guides = window.TRAINER.guides || {};
window.TRAINER.guides.ch11 = {
  chapter: "ch11",
  intro: "This is the longest chapter in Part II and one of the most practical for a first-year resident on call. Shea opens with a poet whose world filled with secret messages, uses him to define psychosis, and then insists on the chapter's governing rule: psychosis is a syndrome, never a diagnosis, so the interviewer's job is to find what is causing it. Seven people carry the teaching, each in a different setting: a man kicking at an invisible creature in the emergency department, a student describing a rape with a faint smile, a woman with a journal of her husband's spies, a 'nervous wreck' whose anxiety is hiding something, a known patient whose rage was not his schizophrenia, a 14-year-old with a dying brain, and a well-dressed woman who once heard an angel. From them you learn to think organic first, to separate schizophrenia from psychotic mood disorders and delusional disorders, to read the soft signs of a psychosis that has not yet declared itself, to explore a delusion without colluding or arguing, to ask the dangerousness questions, and to recognize a delirium before it kills. The practice items, the two simulations ('Something on the floor', 'The neighbor') and the delusion-types drill are built on this chapter.",

  highYield: {
    mustKnow: [
      "Psychosis is a breakdown of perceptual, cognitive, or reasoning function so severe that the person experiences reality very differently from others in the same culture. It is the way thinking happens, not the content, that marks it as psychotic; creative and eccentric thought is not the same thing. And psychosis is a syndrome: always ask what is causing it.",
      "Time course sorts the schizophrenia-spectrum diagnoses: [[Brief psychotic disorder]] (a day to under a month, full recovery), [[Schizophreniform disorder]] (one to six months), schizophrenia (six months or more, with at least a month of active symptoms and a drop in functioning), [[Delusional disorder]] (delusions a month or more with schizophrenia's Criterion A never met), and [[Schizoaffective disorder]] (mood episodes for most of the illness plus at least two weeks of psychosis without them).",
      "Visual hallucinations should make you think organic: substances and withdrawal, endocrine disease, infection, toxins, seizures. Clues that point away from schizophrenia: worse at night and in the dark, brief, moving, impersonal content (snakes, insects, [[Lilliputian hallucinations|little people]]), a prodrome of illusions and geometric shapes, a whole environment that looks distorted, and hallucinations that appear alone rather than with voices. Ask what happens when the patient closes their eyes.",
      "Alcohol and sedative-hypnotic withdrawal are the withdrawals that cause psychosis and the ones that kill. [[Delirium tremens]] follows years of heavy daily drinking, usually begins 24 to 72 hours after the last drink (sometimes a week or more later, or late in a hospital stay once a secret supply dries up), may be preceded by withdrawal seizures, and carries real mortality without treatment. Vital signs belong before the interview of any acutely psychotic patient.",
      "Patients who deny drinking will often admit withdrawal symptoms if you ask matter-of-factly and without implying a 'problem': trouble sleeping since stopping, edginess, nausea, startling at noise. Then stop the interview and get medical care now, not 'later today.'",
      "A florid psychosis that appeared within hours in a previously well person is drug-related until proven otherwise (LSD, PCP, bath salts, synthetic cannabinoids). Any violent patient should raise the question of PCP ([[Three As of PCP|analgesia, amnesia, ataxia]], nystagmus, hypertension). Agitation with insomnia, anger, persecutory delusions, and voices suggests methamphetamine even when denied; its psychosis can outlast the drug.",
      "Medications cause psychosis too, above all anticholinergics in the elderly, often by additive effect across several drugs (cold remedies, hypnotics, antidepressants, antipsychotics, antiparkinsonian agents, antihistamines, ulcer medicines). High-dose oxycodone and hydrocodone can bring terrifying visual hallucinations, sometimes days after surgical discharge. Ask about prescriptions, over-the-counter drugs, and herbs, from the patient and the family.",
      "Interview the police. Circumstances of the find, known substance use, family contact, disorientation or psychosis, drowsiness or unconsciousness, and any fight or head blow. Ask whether an officer was hurt and whether a Taser or baton was used; a struck head in an older patient can mean a subdural bleed.",
      "Schizophrenia's signature is more than delusions. A delusion counts toward schizophrenia only with at least one companion: hallucinations, disorganized speech ([[Derailment|derailment]]), grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, or negative symptoms. [[Deficit (negative) symptoms]] (diminished expression, alogia, avolition, anhedonia, asociality) appear in half to ninety percent of first episodes and often disable more than the [[Positive symptoms]]. Some antipsychotics blunt affect too, so a medicated patient's flat face is not proof of anything.",
      "A delusion is a fixed false belief not shared by the person's culture, held against incontrovertible evidence. Patients vary in their [[Distance from a delusion|distance]] from it; when the distance is maintained the belief has become an [[Overvalued idea]]. [[Bizarre delusion|Bizarre delusions]] (thought insertion, alien control) are typical of schizophrenia; persecutory and jealous delusions need not be bizarre.",
      "Which came first, mood or psychosis? In psychotic depression and mania the psychosis arrives late, after the mood has built to a crescendo; in schizophrenia the psychosis comes early. About half to two thirds of manic patients become psychotic, and an agitated mania can look exactly like schizophrenia, so go back to the first episode, ask the family, read the old records, and take a family history. American psychiatry has historically over-diagnosed schizophrenia and under-diagnosed bipolar disorder, and the error costs patients lithium or antidepressants.",
      "[[Schizoaffective disorder]] fills the gap when mood and psychosis arrive together: striking psychosis like schizophrenia, persistent mood disturbance for most of the illness, and periods of psychosis with normal mood (which pure mood disorders seldom show). Genetics, cognition, and epidemiology increasingly suggest a [[Schizo–bipolar continuum]]; the label matters because it keeps mood stabilizers on the table and warns against a careless antidepressant.",
      "Delusional disorder comes in persecutory, jealous ([[Othello syndrome]]), erotomanic, somatic, grandiose, mixed, and unspecified types; the patient otherwise looks and functions normally, hallucinations are rare and tied to the theme, and the delusion is unshakeable (they come for help with the persecutor, not with themselves). First onset after 40 demands a medical work-up: tumor, medications, endocrine disease, infection, [[Partial complex seizures]], and in the elderly dementia (about a fifth of Alzheimer's patients become paranoid at some point) or [[Paraphrenia]].",
      "The [[Erotomanic delusion]] is that the other person loves the patient, not that the patient loves them, and it survives restraining orders by rationalization. It is dangerous: rejection breeds rage ('if I can't have her, no one can'), and whoever stands between the patient and 'true love' may be at risk. Ask what evidence they have, how they square it with being told never to call, and what they think is stopping the person from admitting it.",
      "Every paranoid delusion, whatever its diagnosis, raises one urgent question: will the patient attack the persecutor in misperceived self-defense? Robinson's two questions (a need to protect yourself from him? a need to take action against him?) and Resnick's [[Confrontation with a paranoid persecutor]] (a concrete scenario: the hit man in the alley, the mailman reaching into his bag) reveal thresholds that a plain 'any thoughts of hurting anyone?' misses.",
      "Psychosis has a life cycle. [[Delusional mood / perception|Delusional mood]] (something is wrong; the world is too vivid) gives way to delusional perception (ordinary things carry personal meaning; [[Ideas of reference]]) and then to delusional ideas (the patient now 'knows'). Clérambault: the psychosis is already old when the delusion begins. A patient can be clinically psychotic before any [[Hard signs of psychosis|hard sign]] exists, and the process fluctuates, so families and staff may see what your interview does not.",
      "[[Soft signs of psychosis]] usually have innocent causes, but they tell you to hunt: mild loosening of associations, idiosyncratic or illogical phrases, unusually intense or angry affect, flashes of inappropriate affect, guardedness, vagueness, preoccupation with a distant event, instant intimacy, long latency, poor eye contact in a non-depressed patient, inappropriate staring. Check the cultural norm before calling a nonverbal behavior a soft sign ([[Kulturbrille]]).",
      "To reach delusional material, tap areas of intense affect and ask, with real interest, what an odd phrase meant. Robinson's three moves: grease the wheels ('tell me more; how did it start?'), uncover the extent and logic ('what has happened so far? why would someone do this?'), and gauge distance ('how do you know?'). What the patient has done about the delusion measures both distance and danger (digging out parasites, carrying a gun).",
      "'Do you believe me?' is common and pivotal. Stay comfortable, keep empathizing, and avoid becoming the arbiter of reality: 'I'm keeping an open mind'; 'I can't decide without more information'; 'my job is to understand your view'; or Shea's favorite, 'it's an unusual story, so I want to understand it better before deciding; tell me more about...' with a return to an emotionally charged detail. Never falsely endorse ('of course I believe you') except, very rarely, to avert imminent violence.",
      "Hallucinations do not always mean psychosis: hypnagogic and hypnopompic experiences, stress and sleep loss, bereavement (nearly half of widowed people sense their spouse), [[Charles Bonnet syndrome]] in the visually impaired, and culturally sanctioned voices and beliefs. The test is whether the phenomenon sits inside a [[Psychotic matrix]] of soft signs before, during, and after it, whether other hard signs turn up, whether a cultural belief has been twisted into a denigrating or accusatory form, and whether it is intensely disturbing and disrupts functioning.",
      "A known patient whose new episode looks different from the usual 'fingerprint' has something new going on. [[Delirium]] is a disturbance of attention and awareness that develops over hours to days and fluctuates; not every delirious patient is disoriented. Two tip-offs in any psychotic patient: a fluctuating level of consciousness (hypoactive, hyperactive, or mixed) and trouble attending. Test it: [[Digit span]] forward and backward, the [[Vigilance test]], constructions, handwriting; or, in a hostile patient, simply watch for lost commands, forgotten questions, startle, and confusion. The [[Confusion Assessment Method]] takes five minutes.",
      "Medical illness can present as a textbook schizophrenia or mania with no delirium at all, as it did in a 14-year-old with viral encephalitis. Rule out the life-threatening causes every time: hypoglycemia, hypertensive encephalopathy, poor oxygenation, encephalitis or meningitis, drugs and toxins, intracranial trauma or pressure, Wernicke's encephalopathy, plus autoimmune encephalitis. A screening physical (vital signs, autonomic signs, heart and lungs, neurological signs and head trauma, the eyes) before anyone leaves the emergency department, and again once an agitated patient has calmed in seclusion.",
      "Not all psychosis is a major psychiatric disorder. [[Micropsychotic episode|Micropsychotic episodes]] (minutes to hours; stress- or drug-triggered; fleeting paranoia, depersonalization, derealization) occur in paranoid, schizotypal, and borderline personality disorders and rarely in decompensating histrionic or narcissistic ones; they begin abruptly, without a prodrome of soft signs, and end abruptly. Intense PTSD flashbacks, including [[Atypical flashback|atypical flashbacks]] of pure fear without memory, can reach psychotic proportions. Partial complex seizures are the 'masquerader par excellence': ask about finding yourself somewhere without knowing how, lapses of consciousness, and odd behavior reported by others, and look for [[Interictal personality change]]."
    ],
    pitfalls: [
      "Writing 'psychotic' in the chart as if it were a diagnosis, and never asking what is producing the psychosis.",
      "Interviewing a sweating, tremulous, tachycardic patient at length about his hallucinations instead of recognizing early delirium tremens and getting him medical care immediately.",
      "Asking a withdrawing patient whether he has a drinking problem (he says no) instead of asking matter-of-factly about sleep, nerves, nausea, and startle since he stopped.",
      "Letting the police leave before you have asked how the patient was found, how he was subdued, and whether anyone's head was struck.",
      "Reading a medicated patient's blunted affect as further proof of schizophrenia, when the medication may be producing it and the original diagnosis may have been wrong.",
      "Diagnosing schizophrenia from a delusion alone, or missing a delusional disorder because the patient seemed 'too normal' until the topic came up.",
      "Trusting a patient's denial of voices when the family describes nightly arguments with 'her' in the basement; psychotic process fluctuates and patients know that voices buy a hospital bed.",
      "Calling an agitated psychotic mania schizophrenia without going back to the first episode to learn which came first, mood or psychosis.",
      "Hearing the patient's wish for the person they are stalking and missing that the delusion is the other person's love, and that the spouse in the way may be in danger.",
      "Asking a paranoid patient only 'any thoughts of hurting anyone?' and accepting the honest 'no' from someone who would shoot the mailman the moment he reached into his bag.",
      "Agreeing with a delusion to keep the peace ('of course I believe you'), then being asked to call the police about it.",
      "Arguing with the delusion instead, and losing the patient along with the rest of the history.",
      "Dismissing the soft signs (an odd phrase, a giggle, a preoccupation with a distant event, a long pause before each answer) in an anxious outpatient, and labeling the prodrome of a psychosis as generalized anxiety or hysteria.",
      "Calling a culturally sanctioned hallucination or belief psychotic because it is unfamiliar to you, or calling a truly psychotic distortion of such a belief 'just cultural.'",
      "Assuming a chronic patient's violent relapse is his old illness, admitting him to seclusion by phone, and missing the tricyclic overdose that stops his breathing half an hour later.",
      "Being reassured by a delirious patient who looks fine on morning rounds; the night nurse saw the delirium, and brain damage accrues between your visits.",
      "Discharging a patient with new psychotic symptoms from the emergency department, or admitting an agitated one to seclusion, without a screening physical examination.",
      "Treating fifteen minutes of a voice two years ago as schizophrenia, and robbing a patient with partial complex seizures or a personality disorder of the right treatment."
    ],
    phrases: [
      { say: "When you see them, what happens if you close your eyes?", when: "Sorting organic from schizophrenic visual hallucinations: drug- and illness-related images are often more vivid in the dark or with the eyes shut; schizophrenia's are not." },
      { say: "Since you cut back on the drinking, has your sleep been off? A lot of people use alcohol to get to sleep, and without it they can't. Any shakiness, or feeling sick to your stomach, or jumping at noises?", when: "Uncovering withdrawal from a patient who denies drinking, with no suggestion of a 'problem.' Composed." },
      { say: "Looks like you had your hands full tonight. Did any of your officers get hurt? We'd be glad to check them over. And in getting him down, did he take a knock to the head by any chance?", when: "The police interview: collegial, matter-of-fact, and quietly raising the possibility of head trauma. Composed." },
      { say: "Thinking back to the very first time anything like this happened, which came first: the change in your mood and energy, or the voices and the fears?", when: "Dating mood against psychosis in the first episode, the question that separates schizophrenia from psychotic mood disorder. Composed." },
      { say: "What makes you think she loves you, when she has told you never to contact her again? What do you think is stopping her from saying so openly?", when: "Testing for a true erotomanic delusion; if the answer to the second question is a spouse or partner, that person may be in danger. Composed." },
      { say: "Do you feel you need to protect yourself from him? Have you felt you might have to take action against him, or harm him?", when: "Robinson's two dangerousness questions for any paranoid delusion, asked after rapport is established." },
      { say: "Suppose you were on your porch and he walked up the path and reached into his bag. What would you do?", when: "Resnick's confrontation with a paranoid persecutor: a concrete scenario that reveals the threshold for pre-emptive violence. Composed." },
      { say: "I'm interested in what you just said. How did all this start? What has happened so far? Why would someone want to do this to you? How do you know that's what's going on?", when: "Robinson's sequence: grease the wheels, uncover the extent and logic, gauge distance from the delusion." },
      { say: "It's an unusual story, and I want to understand it properly before I decide anything. Help me see exactly what the parasites are doing to you now.", when: "Answering 'Do you believe me?' without endorsing or arguing, then returning to an affectively charged detail. Composed." },
      { say: "Some of my patients worry that something is getting into their home through the walls or windows, a gas, a poison, even radiation. Any worries like that?", when: "A normalized screen for the partition delusion that over half of patients with paraphrenia report and seldom volunteer. Composed." },
      { say: "I'm going to say some numbers; wait until I point to you, then repeat them back. We'll start short.", when: "A digit span that tests immediate recall rather than echoing, said in a steady rhythm so the digits cannot be chunked like a phone number. Composed." },
      { say: "Have you ever found yourself somewhere and not known how you got there? Any spells of losing consciousness? Has anyone told you they've seen you doing odd things you don't remember?", when: "Screening for partial complex seizures in any patient with psychotic symptoms." }
    ]
  },

  sections: [
    {
      id: "defining",
      title: "A plank in reason: what psychosis is and is not",
      lede: "A poet's last pages, a working definition, the rule that psychosis is a syndrome, and the diagnoses that time course separates.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Shea opens with Emily Dickinson's image of a plank in reason breaking, and then with Gérard de Nerval, a gifted nineteenth-century poet whose first psychotic break came in 1841 and who, fourteen years later, was found hanged in a Paris alley with fragments of an autobiographical work in his pocket. Summarized from the chapter: in those pages he describes fellow patients in an asylum garden whom he believed controlled the stars, ordinary conversations charged with mystical meaning, a conviction that an error in the universe's numbers explained all human suffering, and voices from plants and insects warning and encouraging him, messages he alone could understand. The last point is the clue: as psychotic process intensifies, the person's world becomes progressively unique to them and recedes from the world as others witness it." },
        { type: "p", text: "That gives the working definition: psychosis is a breakdown of the perceptual, cognitive, or reasoning functions of the mind to the point that the individual experiences reality very differently from other people within the same culture. Two qualifications follow. First, psychotic process can be exciting and even beautiful, but it is almost always accompanied in the end by intensely painful fear, a sense of impending catastrophe, and sometimes a crushing responsibility (de Nerval's inability to correct the cosmic error may have been what convinced him he had failed God and humanity). Second, creative and eccentric thinking can resemble psychosis without being it; what marks the process as psychotic is less the content of the thinking than the way the thinking occurs." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Psychosis is not a diagnosis", text: "Psychosis is a syndrome that can be produced by many disorders. It is never enough to note that a patient looks psychotic; the task is to find what is causing it. For schizophrenia and psychotic bipolar disorder, timely recognition means earlier treatment, lower antipsychotic doses, and less suffering; for delirium and central nervous system infection it can mean life itself." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Diagnosis", "Duration", "What else it requires"], rows: [
          ["[[Brief psychotic disorder]]", "At least a day, less than a month", "One or more of delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior (at least one from the first three); full return to prior functioning; note a precipitating stressor if present ('brief reactive psychosis' in older literature)"],
          ["[[Schizophreniform disorder]]", "At least a month, less than six months (prodrome, active, and residual phases included)", "Meets schizophrenia's symptom criterion with mood and schizoaffective disorders excluded; marked decline in functioning not required; often provisional, becoming schizophrenia if it lasts"],
          ["Schizophrenia", "Continuous signs for six months or more, including at least a month of active symptoms", "Two or more of delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, negative symptoms (at least one from the first three); marked fall in functioning; mood episodes absent or brief relative to the illness; not substance or medical; course and catatonia specifiers after a year"],
          ["[[Delusional disorder]]", "Delusions for a month or more", "Schizophrenia's symptom criterion never met; hallucinations, if any, not prominent and tied to the theme; functioning not markedly impaired and behavior not obviously odd apart from the delusion; mood episodes brief"],
          ["[[Schizoaffective disorder]]", "Mood episodes present for the majority of the illness", "A major mood episode concurrent with schizophrenia's symptom criterion, plus two weeks or more of delusions or hallucinations without a mood episode at some point; bipolar or depressive type"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Shea reprints the DSM-5 criteria for schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, and delirium in full; this guide summarizes them, and you should read the originals in the book or the manual. Note the course specifiers (first versus multiple episodes, acute, partial or full remission, continuous) that remind us a person with schizophrenia need not be psychotic continuously." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch11", label: "Quiz: Chapter 11" }]
    },

    {
      id: "visual-withdrawal",
      title: "Mr. Williams: visual hallucinations and the withdrawal that kills",
      lede: "The first clue to an organic psychosis, the difference between an illusion and a hallucination, and delirium tremens.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Williams", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 33-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by three irritated police officers who describe him bolting from something only he can see. He is calm at first, sweating, finishing a meal, oriented, in torn street clothes; as he talks his speech becomes tangential with moments of derailment and he grows distractible. He denies drinking or drugs but tells a vague story about a creature following him. Mid-interview his eyes widen, he stares at the floor, kicks at nothing, and shouts at the clinician to get rid of the thing. Asked to point to it, he reaches toward the empty space in front of his feet and stops: he will go no closer. Soon he reports bugs on his skin and live wires on the floor shocking him. His pulse is 100 and his temperature slightly raised. The clinician cuts the interview short for a physical examination and medical management; the officers report years of severe drinking and 'any drug he could get'; he has had DTs before. On diazepam the psychosis vanishes within hours." },
        { type: "p", text: "The first clue was the visual hallucination. Schizophrenia can produce them, but auditory hallucinations are far more common there, whereas medical causes (substance intoxication and withdrawal, endocrine disease, infection, toxins, seizures) frequently produce vivid visual hallucinations with or without voices. No feature is decisive, but Shea, drawing on Fish and others, collects the tendencies." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Visual hallucinations in medical or drug psychosis", items: [
            "More often at night, briefer, more often moving",
            "Little personal meaning: snakes, insects, trains, [[Lilliputian hallucinations|little people]] (sometimes with pleasure, early in DTs)",
            "More vivid in a darkened room or with the eyes shut",
            "A prodrome of illusions, simple geometric figures, changes in color, size, shape, and movement",
            "The whole environment distorted, with many illusions (hallucinogens)",
            "Once concrete, terrifyingly real: the patient points, eyes it warily, backs away",
            "Tactile hallucinations and illusions (mice, lice, bugs on the skin) common in DTs"
          ] },
          b: { title: "Visual hallucinations in schizophrenia", items: [
            "Usually accompanied by auditory or other hallucinations; seldom alone",
            "Concrete content: faces, body parts, whole figures, often personally meaningful (a dead relative)",
            "Seen with the eyes open; little difference with the eyes closed",
            "Appear suddenly, without a prodrome of illusions",
            "Superimposed on an otherwise normal environment, or appearing with the surroundings absent"
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "Two practical habits follow. Ask what happens when the patient closes their eyes, and, with an inpatient, ask the nurses whether the hallucinations are worse at night. Keep the vocabulary straight as well: a hallucination arises from open space with no triggering stimulus (Mr. Williams's creature in front of his feet); an [[Illusion vs hallucination|illusion]] is triggered by a real object, like the patient on a bus who watched a stranger's face twist and his eyes shatter and bleed. Illusions can be as striking and terrifying as hallucinations." },
        { type: "p", text: "Mr. Williams had a substance withdrawal delirium, the condition traditionally called [[Delirium tremens]]. Among withdrawal states, alcohol and the sedative-hypnotics are the ones most likely to cause psychotic features and the ones most likely to kill; their withdrawal is far more dangerous than withdrawal from heroin or amphetamines, with historic mortality in hospitalized DTs as high as 15% and much lower with good management. Withdrawal progresses from sleep disturbance, nausea, anxiety, over-alertness, tremulousness, and a curious sharpening of the senses toward DTs, which typically lasts five to ten days. The syndrome needs a long history of heavy daily drinking (on the order of a pint of spirits, or several pints of wine or many of beer, every day for months, usually years), rarely occurs before 30, and reflects unchecked autonomic compensation once the alcohol stops: raised pulse, temperature, and breathing rate, normal or high blood pressure, twitching, sweating, and tremor severe enough to impair walking." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Even a patient who denies drinking will often admit withdrawal symptoms if asked matter-of-factly, without the suggestion of a 'personal problem': sleep trouble since stopping (many people use alcohol to sleep), feeling edgy and unable to relax, nausea, being startled by noise or upset by people moving or talking loudly nearby.",
          "Survey the physiological signs during the interview, and have vital signs taken before the interview of any acutely psychotic patient.",
          "Withdrawal seizures ('rum fits') usually occur in the first two days after stopping; more than a third of patients who have them go on to DTs. DTs usually begin 24 to 72 hours after the last drink but can appear a week or more later.",
          "In hospital, DTs can appear late: a friend who was smuggling alcohol stops visiting, a well-off patient has lied about consumption, or surgery has delayed the onset. Suspect withdrawal in any inpatient who becomes psychotic at any point in a stay, especially with elevated vital signs.",
          "These patients need prompt medical attention. If you are not a physician, arrange for one immediately; an appointment later in the day is inadequate and dangerous."
        ] }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c11p1", label: "Practice: uncovering withdrawal" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-floor", label: "Simulation: Something on the floor" }]
    },

    {
      id: "drugs-police",
      title: "Intoxication, medications, herbs, and the interview with the police",
      lede: "The agents that produce psychosis within hours, the medications that produce delirium in the elderly, and the six things to learn from the officers.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The second way substances cause psychosis is intoxication, and the list is long: methamphetamine, LSD and other hallucinogens, cannabis, cocaine and crack, PCP, and newer agents. Shea's example is PCP, an anesthetic from the 1950s. Intoxicated patients may be markedly psychotic or not psychotic at all, and they can be extremely violent, so any violent patient should raise the question of PCP. Even low doses produce the [[Three As of PCP]]: analgesia (which permits self-mutilation such as eye gouging), amnesia, and ataxia. Behavior can be bizarre (public nudity, crawling on all fours); paranoia, disorientation, and both auditory and visual hallucinations occur. The examination shows nystagmus, hypertension in over half, usually small pupils (large if an anticholinergic was also taken), increased tone and salivation; high doses produce lethargy or coma rather than agitation. If PCP is even remotely suspected, have security informed and nearby. 'Bath salts' (a shifting mixture of synthetic stimulants, once legal) and synthetic cannabinoids ('spice') can produce PCP-like states ranging from nudity to extreme violence." },
        { type: "p", text: "Methamphetamine deserves its own paragraph. Chronic use produces a state that can look exactly like paranoid schizophrenia or a psychotic dysphoric mania; persecutory delusions and auditory hallucinations are the commonest symptoms, and they can persist after the drug stops, along with cognitive impairment, social instability, and more lifetime suicide attempts. Any patient presenting agitated, sleepless, angry, paranoid, and hearing voices should be considered a possible methamphetamine user and screened, even when use is adamantly denied." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "A full-blown psychosis that appeared in a matter of hours should make you very suspicious of a drug; schizophrenia develops over days, weeks, or months.",
          "The patient may not know they were drugged (something slipped into a drink or sprinkled on a joint); ask the friends who were there.",
          "Always consider two possibilities: more than one street drug, and a medication rather than a street drug."
        ] },
        { type: "case", title: "A few extra tablets", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A young woman with chronic paranoid schizophrenia, doing well on the unit, returned from a home pass apprehensive and within half an hour was racing down the hall pursued by small dragons. Dilated, sluggish pupils, a dry mouth, and a fast pulse told the story: she had taken extra benztropine, the anticholinergic prescribed for her antipsychotic's side effects, and tipped into an anticholinergic delirium." },
        { type: "p", text: "Anticholinergic agents precipitate delirium quickly, the elderly are especially vulnerable, and the effect is additive across drugs that are each only mildly anticholinergic: some over-the-counter hypnotics and cold medicines, certain antidepressants and antipsychotics, antiparkinsonian drugs, some peptic ulcer medications, and antihistamines. Elicit the medication history from patient and family. Two more categories: the semisynthetic opioids (oxycodone, hydrocodone) are not usually psychotogenic, but at high doses they can cause vivid, terrifying visual hallucinations with agitation and anxiety, sometimes appearing days after a surgical patient has gone home; and herbs and 'natural' products, including the anticholinergic nightshade family (belladonna, jimsonweed, mandrake, henbane) whose potions may lie behind the medieval witches' reports of night flight. An unexplained acute psychosis should prompt a question about herbs." },
        { type: "p", text: "When the police bring a patient, one of the most important interviews is with the officers, and the first skill is making the time for it when everyone is harried. Establish six things: the circumstances in which the patient was found; whether the patient is a known drinker or drug user; whether the officers know the family and whether the family has been contacted; whether the patient seemed disoriented or psychotic; whether the patient has been drowsy or unconscious; and whether there was a fight that could have involved a blow to the head. Ask early whether any officer was injured; it matters medically and it builds the collaboration you will need for years. Find out how the patient was subdued, including whether a Taser or baton was used, and whether the head was struck justly or otherwise, because a subdural hematoma or intracranial bleed can be the psychosis, especially in an older patient. Most officers use force only when they must; angry countertransference toward the police only costs you information. Crisis Intervention Team programs (the Memphis model) and similar efforts train officers to handle psychiatric patients effectively and compassionately, and are worth knowing wherever you work." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "At the nurses' station", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Looks like you had your hands full tonight." },
          { who: "Police", t: "Three of us to get him in the car. He kept diving at the ground like something was after him." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "He's wound up; he may be on something. Did any of your people get hurt? We're happy to check them over.", tag: "collegial, and medically relevant" },
          { who: "Police", t: "We're fine, thanks." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Did you have to Taser him, or was it hands on?" },
          { who: "Police", t: "Hands on. No Taser." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "In the scuffle, any chance he caught a knock on the head? If he did, we'd want to make sure there's no small fracture or bleed under it.", tag: "the head-blow question, with the reason attached" },
          { who: "Police", t: "Honestly, it was fast. He went down hard on the pavement at one point. You might want to look." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "We will. Thanks for bringing him in; you did a good job with a tough one." }
        ], note: "Composed. The clinician gets all the pivotal facts, reminds the officers unobtrusively about head injury, and leaves them with respect rather than suspicion." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c11p6", label: "Practice: interviewing police" }]
    },

    {
      id: "schizophrenia",
      title: "Mr. Walker: schizophrenia's signature",
      lede: "Affect, delusions, deficit symptoms, the mother's interview, and why a calm patient may still be psychotic.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Walker", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A gaunt 20-year-old student, referred from his college counseling center after the winter break, is interviewed on the unit by a social worker. He nods shyly, speaks softly and slowly, and his face and voice show almost no expression as he describes three filthy women who enter his house at night to force sex on him, one of them 'that devil Miss Brown,' and a party at which men tied him to a chair and raped him. A faint smile crosses his face as he says it. No rape occurred. There is no derailment, tangentiality, blocking, or illogical thought; he is alert and oriented; neither he nor his family reports drugs. The family dates the oddness back almost a year, with vague accusations about Miss Brown through the summer. The clinician feels uncomfortable and somewhat frightened." },
        { type: "p", text: "Start with the blandness. Affect is the manner in which emotion and spontaneity are expressed through the face, and a useful interviewing habit is to ask yourself whether the patient seems appropriately disturbed while describing something traumatic. Mr. Walker showed almost no fear, anxiety, or anger: mildly reduced affect is called [[Restricted affect|restricted]], moderately reduced [[Blunted affect|blunted]], essentially absent [[Flat affect|flat]]. He also showed [[Inappropriate affect]]: rape victims do not smile as they describe the assault. The combination of flattening and inappropriateness is common in schizophrenia and is one of the things that produce an unsettling response in the clinician. One warning: some antipsychotics blunt affect as a side effect, so a patient mislabeled with schizophrenia by a previous clinician may present with medication-induced flatness that the next clinician reads as confirmation, perpetuating the error." },
        { type: "p", text: "More striking still, he is delusional. A delusion is a false belief firmly held and not shared by others in the patient's culture, persisting against incontrovertible evidence. Patients vary over time in their [[Distance from a delusion|distance]] (insight): when enough distance is gained and kept that the person can see the belief may be untrue, it is no longer a delusion but an [[Overvalued idea]]. Schizophrenia's delusions are often [[Bizarre delusion|bizarre]] (alien or demonic control of the body, thoughts inserted or withdrawn) or magical, grandiose, or hyper-religious; paranoid schizophrenia produces persecutory and jealous delusions, like Mr. Walker's, which need not be bizarre at all." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Schizophrenia", items: [
            "Delusions are only part of the process: at least one companion symptom (hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, negative symptoms)",
            "Formal thought disorder may show as incoherence or marked [[Derailment|loosening of associations]]",
            "Significant decline in social or occupational functioning over time",
            "Not every patient with schizophrenia has delusions"
          ] },
          b: { title: "Delusional disorder", items: [
            "Delusions essentially alone; infrequent hallucinations, if any, tied directly to the delusional system (the neighbor's threats heard occasionally; parasites felt in the abdomen)",
            "Surprisingly good baseline functioning at home and work",
            "Reasonably normal, appropriately reactive affect",
            "Explored in the Ms. Hastings section below"
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "The DSM-IV's lasting contribution, continued in the DSM-5, was naming the negative symptoms. Earlier systems emphasized the [[Positive symptoms]], the unwanted excess that most people never experience (hallucinations, delusions). Schizophrenia also removes normal functions, the [[Deficit (negative) symptoms]], Shea's preferred term: diminished affect, alogia (less speech and less interest in speaking), avolition (less drive and inability to sustain interest), anhedonia, asociality, and for some clinicians anergia. Rado's summary of the deficit syndrome: negative symptoms are present in 50 to 90% of first episodes, go with more cognitive disorganization and poorer insight, and predict worse psychosocial, vocational, and recreational functioning, more anxiety, lower felt competence, and poorer interpersonal skills. They are often more disabling than the voices, and devastating to self-esteem." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "What he does in the basement", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What does he do down there all day?" },
          { who: "Mother", t: "Talks to her. I hear a woman's voice coming up the stairs, except it's him doing it. At night it turns into shouting matches. I don't go down." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "When he's upstairs with you, does he ever look as if he's hearing something?", tag: "asking the family for the hard sign the patient withheld" },
          { who: "Mother", t: "He mumbles like he's answering someone. But some days he's almost himself, and then last night he came up the stairs with a kitchen knife screaming at me to make them stop." }
        ], note: "Composed. The patient never admitted hallucinations; his mother supplied them, along with the knife. Psychotic patients withhold critical information for their own reasons, and families gratefully fill in the gaps." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "The psychosis you catch is the psychosis of that hour", text: "Psychotic process fluctuates, even in schizophrenia. Many interviewers have been lulled into concluding that a patient is not psychotic because the interview fell in a quiet period. Listen to the family, and remember that the patient's rational side knows that telling the 'shrink' about voices is a quick ticket into the hospital." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch11", label: "Quiz: Chapter 11" }]
    },

    {
      id: "mood-vs-psychosis",
      title: "Which came first? Mood disorders, schizophrenia, and the schizoaffective middle",
      lede: "Criterion D, the crescendo rule, the schizo–bipolar continuum, and why the label changes the prescription.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Schizophrenia's Criterion D excludes mood disorders with psychotic features, so every psychotic patient needs the mood history. The organizing fact is timing. In major depression or bipolar disorder with psychotic features (mood-congruent or incongruent), the psychosis generally appears a considerable time after the mood disorder began, as if the depressive or manic process built to a crescendo that bloomed into psychosis; in schizophrenia the psychosis is usually an early part of the process, predating the worst mood symptoms. Errors run in both directions. Most people with schizophrenia have an affective syndrome at some point, anhedonia is itself a negative symptom, and some presentations are confusing mixtures; conversely American psychiatry has tended to over-diagnose schizophrenia and under-diagnose bipolar disorder, an error that is not benign because the mislabeled patient might have responded to lithium or an antidepressant." },
        { type: "p", text: "Timing matters most in the differential between a manic episode and schizophrenia, because an agitated mania can look exactly like a psychotic presentation of schizophrenia. A patient can be manic without being psychotic (energy, unstable mood, agitation, little need for sleep, pressured speech); mania becomes psychotic only when reality contact breaks, which happens in roughly 50 to 70% of manic patients, and again the psychosis tends to come well after the mood symptoms. To use that difference you must learn about the very first episode." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "Ask family members and read prior records to establish which came first, psychotic or mood symptoms.",
          "In a patient with many prior breaks, return to the first break and reconstruct the chronology of depressive or manic symptoms.",
          "Take a detailed family history; many blood relatives with mood disorders hint that the patient sits toward the mood end of the spectrum.",
          "Before diagnosing schizophrenia in a clearly psychotic patient, look carefully for a history of mood symptoms that would suggest schizoaffective disorder."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "What of the patient whose psychosis and mood disturbance arrive together, or the patient with classic schizophrenia whose prodrome included months of low-grade dysthymic mood? [[Schizoaffective disorder]] fills the gap: a major mood episode concurrent with schizophrenia's symptom criterion, at least two weeks of delusions or hallucinations without a mood episode somewhere in the illness, and mood symptoms present for the majority of its total duration (bipolar or depressive type). Shea admits the definition is vague enough for a campaign speech, and argues that the vagueness is honest: categories are labels for commonly observed patterns, not real-life entities. Tsuang proposed in the 1970s that schizoaffective disorder was heterogeneous, with affective and schizophrenic subtypes and no distinct genetics, and he has been vindicated by genetic, cognitive, neurobiological, and epidemiological overlap between schizophrenia and psychotic bipolar disorder, even in their prodromes. The emerging picture is a [[Schizo–bipolar continuum]] or 'psychosis spectrum' with pure schizophrenia and pure bipolar disorder at the poles and the schizoaffective states between." },
        { type: "p", text: "Clinically, the schizoaffective patient has the striking psychosis of schizophrenia plus persistent, significant mood disturbance, and differs from the psychotic mood disorders in having periods of marked psychosis with fairly normal mood, which pure mood disorders seldom show; they differ from classic schizophrenia in having long stretches of striking mood symptoms, early and throughout, often without psychosis. If psychosis begins to appear without concurrent mood symptoms for most of the illness, the diagnosis changes to schizophrenia. None of this is academic. A diagnosis of schizophrenia makes later clinicians less likely to consider lithium or valproate; a schizoaffective label keeps the mood component in view, warns against adding an antidepressant that could unleash mania when the type is bipolar, and may carry a better prognosis." },
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Walker's formulation", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A year of downhill course met the time criterion for schizophrenia; the family recalled intermittent, unstriking depression without a marked mood disturbance before the delusions or in the early months. Pending the medical work-up that any first psychosis needs (methamphetamine, thyroid disease), the working formulation was schizophrenia, rule out schizoaffective disorder, with no personality or medical diagnoses." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "drill", id: "mood-pictures", label: "Drill: Mood presentations" }]
    },

    {
      id: "delusional-disorders",
      title: "Ms. Hastings: delusional disorders, dangerousness, and paraphrenia",
      lede: "The seven types, the erotomanic trap, Robinson's and Resnick's dangerousness questions, the medical rule-out after 40, and late-onset psychosis.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Hastings", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 57-year-old woman, a little bedraggled and indignant, opens her first visit to a group psychology practice by asking for help with her husband. Her speech is fluent, even eloquent, with no derailment, illogic, or bizarre ideas. She describes his infidelity and his hiring of men with 'conventional spy tools' to harass her into insanity so that he can obtain a divorce; her own mother may be in on it. She has a journal of drawings and schedules documenting the 'goons next door.' No hallucinations, no prior psychiatric history, no depressive symptoms beyond being understandably edgy. She smokes heavily, has peptic ulcer disease and chronic bronchitis, and a new hacking cough." },
        { type: "p", text: "Unlike Mr. Walker, Ms. Hastings seems convincingly normal: her affect is appropriately upset for someone who believes she is the target of foul play, she has no hallucinations, no formal thought disorder, and no psychosis before 50. Her only psychopathology is a concrete delusional system, which cannot by itself meet criteria for schizophrenia. She has a delusional disorder, a group in which patients look so normal that nothing is suspected until the topic arises, after which they describe ramifications and subplots to gratify any soap-opera fan. The delusions are unshakeable, the patients do not believe anything is wrong with them (she came for her husband's problem, not her own), and they are often frustratingly resistant to therapy. Any of these delusions can occur in schizophrenia or delirium; it is their presence as the only sign of psychopathology that makes the diagnosis." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Type", "Central theme", "Notes for the interviewer"], rows: [
          ["Persecutory (paranoid)", "A person, organization, or bizarre entity is thwarting, harming, spying on, poisoning, maligning, or trying to kill the patient or loved ones", "Content follows the culture (webcams and phone tracking now, both of which hackers can in fact do); far more often seen as part of schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, psychotic depression, or substance psychosis than as a delusional disorder"],
          ["Jealous ([[Othello syndrome]])", "The spouse or lover is unfaithful", "Potential for violence toward partner or rival"],
          ["Erotomanic (Clérambault's syndrome)", "Another person, often of higher status, is in love with the patient", "The delusion is the other's love, not the patient's; survives denials and restraining orders by rationalization; the 'Hollywood stalker'; rejection can turn to 'if I can't have her, no one can,' and whoever stands between them may be targeted"],
          ["Somatic (monosymptomatic hypochondriacal psychosis)", "A serious bodily illness, infestation, foul odor, or malfunctioning part, held with delusional conviction", "Differs from hypochondriasis by being unshakeable, sometimes with a belief in a cover-up; preoccupation with a body part's appearance is more likely body dysmorphic disorder (OCD spectrum); some progress to schizophrenia (Shea's patient brought a skull to demonstrate his jaw's defects)"],
          ["Grandiose", "A great unrecognized talent, insight, or discovery", ""],
          ["Mixed", "No single theme predominates", "Ms. Hastings: jealous and persecutory themes equally central"],
          ["Unspecified", "Theme cannot be determined or fits none of the above", "Also 'with bizarre content' as a specifier"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Folie à deux]] (shared psychotic disorder) is the rare case in which a second person, usually dependent and suggestible, is induced into the delusion of a dominant partner; the induced delusion may crumble when the pair are separated. For the erotomanic patient, ask gently and without accusation what evidence they have that the person loves them, how they put that together with being told never to make contact or with a court order, and what they think is stopping the person from admitting their love. If the answer to the last question is a spouse or partner, remember that the patient may be contemplating murder as a favor to the beloved." },
        { type: "p", text: "Whatever the diagnosis, the most urgent task with a persecutory delusion is to learn whether the patient intends to harm or kill the persecutor as proactive self-defense. Robinson's two questions do this sensitively: does the patient feel a need to protect themselves against the person, and have they felt a need to take action against or harm them. Resnick's [[Confrontation with a paranoid persecutor]] goes further, after rapport is carefully established, because paranoid patients fear commitment and police. Describe a concrete scenario and ask what the patient would do: a man dressed like a hit man walking toward you in an alley with a bulge in his jacket. One patient says nothing, the mafia is too powerful; another says he would draw his revolver and fire. Both would honestly answer 'no' to a bare question about thoughts of killing anyone, yet their thresholds for killing in misperceived self-defense differ completely, and the answer bears on hospitalization and on warning a potential victim." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "On the porch", lines: [
          { who: "Pt.", t: "My wife and the mail carrier are poisoning me. The food tastes wrong, and he looks at me like he'd kill me." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "How did you come to know that's what's happening?", tag: "extent and logic" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "She's gone cold on me. I went to the police and they wanted proof. So I started carrying." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Suppose you're sitting on your porch and he walks up the path and starts to take something out of his bag. What would you do?", tag: "the confrontation scenario" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "I'd have to shoot. They're getting impatient that the poison's too slow." }
        ], note: "Composed, after Resnick's model. Note what the scenario revealed that 'any thoughts of hurting anyone?' would not have." },
        { type: "p", text: "With any delusional disorder, rule out a psychotic disorder due to a medical condition, especially when the delusions first appeared after 40: brain tumors (malignant gliomas in middle age, metastases in the elderly), medications, endocrine disorders, infections, and [[Partial complex seizures]]. In the elderly consider dementia; roughly 20% of patients with Alzheimer's disease show paranoid symptoms at some point, suspiciousness early and delusions later. Ms. Hastings's formulation: delusional disorder, mixed type, rule out a psychotic disorder due to another medical condition; personality deferred; peptic ulcer disease, chronic bronchitis, and rule out lung carcinoma, because the medical list should name the entities the interview has actually raised." },
        { type: "p", text: "Some older patients present with concrete paranoid delusions plus auditory hallucinations and other schizophrenia-like features, as if with a late-onset schizophrenia. The DSM-5 has no upper age limit for schizophrenia, but it is unclear whether these late psychoses are the same illness, and the available labels (delusional disorder if hallucinations are not prominent; 'unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder' otherwise) are, Shea complains, grab-bags for a syndrome that is neither uncommon nor atypical (at least 10% of psychotic admissions over 60 in one study). European psychiatry calls it [[Paraphrenia]], and Shea prefers the term: well-organized paranoid delusions (grandiose, erotic, and somatic ones less often) usually with auditory hallucinations, often in several modalities; a prodrome of months or years of suspiciousness, irritability, seclusiveness, and odd behavior; no affective blunting or formal thought disorder even when grossly psychotic; preserved personality, fewer negative symptoms, less deterioration, and a reasonable response to antipsychotics; six- to ten-fold more common in women; and hearing loss in 15 to 40%, which may feed the misinterpretation of others' conversation. The delusions tend to be mundane and believable (the neighbor breaking in, the grocer after the pension checks) rather than aliens and spies. Over half report a [[Partition delusion]], that people, objects, gases, or radiation are entering the home through impermeable walls, perhaps because so many live alone; it goes unmentioned unless you ask, with a normalization or by tying it to the persecutor already named ('do you feel Mr. Roberts has found a way to pass something dangerous into your house, or to see through your walls?')." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "drill", id: "delusion-types", label: "Drill: Delusional disorder subtypes" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c11p3", label: "Practice: assessing dangerousness" }, { kind: "sim", id: "sim-neighbor", label: "Simulation: The neighbor" }]
    },

    {
      id: "life-cycle",
      title: "Ms. Fay: the life cycle of a psychosis and its soft signs",
      lede: "Hard signs, clinical versus diagnostic psychosis, the three phases of a delusion, the soft signs that say 'hunt,' and how to tap them.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Fay", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A 23-year-old divorced mother of two, living with her mother, returns to a community mental health center two weeks after being diagnosed with a fairly severe generalized anxiety disorder. She is a 'nervous wreck': wriggling, picking at her nails, avoiding eye contact, telling the male clinician that he makes her nervous. She cannot sleep or relax; her speech is mildly pressured and evasive in a way that frustrates the interviewer; she flatly denies delusions and hallucinations; she is preoccupied with a distant episode of sexual abuse she does not want to discuss; and she giggles coyly at odd moments, as though she would burrow under her chair to escape scrutiny." },
        { type: "p", text: "Anxiety is one of the most frequent early signs of a developing psychosis, so hers deserves careful exploration. Start with definitions. The [[Hard signs of psychosis]] are hallucinations, delusions, and a formal thought disorder (moderate to severe loosening of associations or other problems forming thought); clinically, gross disorganization, gross disorientation, and bizarre behavior join the list. In a strict diagnostic sense a patient is not called psychotic without at least one hard sign, a conservative convention Shea endorses because it prevents loose labeling (the schizotypal patient called schizophrenic). But in the clinical sense, experiencing the world strikingly differently from one's culture, a patient can be psychotic without any hard sign, especially early and when the process fluctuates. Understanding how a psychosis unfolds resolves the paradox." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "[[Delusional mood / perception|Delusional mood]] (Lopez-Ibor): something is not quite right. Perceptions intensify; lights seem deeper, sounds more numerous; details never noticed before take on significance; there is an unsettling sense that something ominous is coming, though life can also feel refreshingly vivid.",
          "Delusional perception (Kurt Schneider): the perception is sensorially normal but its interpretation is distorted. Anxiety snowballs; the environment's details are felt to relate directly to the patient; [[Ideas of reference]] appear (people are talking about me, but why?). The world is at once personalized and terrifying. Functioning may already be changing: avoiding people, checking behaviors, re-reading social media posts for hidden messages, ruminating all night. The patient is already psychotic in the clinical sense, without hard signs.",
          "Delusional ideas: suspicions crystallize into concrete beliefs. The patient 'knows' what is being said and why; paranoia becomes elaborate and entrenched delusion; the hard signs flower. Clérambault: the psychosis is already old when the delusion begins, almost as if the delusion were an answer to why the world has felt so ominous."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Because psychotic process fluctuates, patients move in and out of these phases, delusional one day and not the next, especially in a first episode or when a psychosis is breaking through medication. The interviewer therefore pays keen attention to evidence of the less obvious phases, the [[Soft signs of psychosis]], and when they are present hunts carefully for the hard ones. Soft signs do not mean the patient is psychotic; usually they are produced by anxiety, an immature interpersonal style, or idiosyncratic habits, and a mild loosening of associations may be an ingrained schizotypal style. Their value is as a signal to search. Intense anger is the clearest example: the vast majority of angry patients are not psychotic, but intense anger in a first interview should always prompt a careful look for psychotic process." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "Mild or infrequent formal thought disorder: slight loosening, occasional illogical thought, idiosyncratic speech, mild to moderate tangentiality",
          "Unusually intense affect; angry or agitated affect; infrequent glimpses of inappropriate affect",
          "Guardedness or suspiciousness; vagueness",
          "Preoccupation with an incident from the distant past",
          "Immediate discussion of personal details as if the interviewer were an old friend",
          "Long latency before answering; poor eye contact in a patient who does not seem depressed; inappropriate staring",
          "Check cultural norms for eye contact and other nonverbal behavior before scoring them, or the [[Kulturbrille]] turns a normal gesture into a sign"
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Patients do not reliably volunteer hallucinations and delusions; often you must ask for the specific symptom. Two gates open the psychotic world. First, when a patient seems unusually charged or anxious about a topic, follow it with interest and clarifying questions rather than structure, because as affect rises defenses fall and delusional material surfaces; interviewers routinely run over these areas and rob themselves of the natural entry. Second, when a patient uses an illogical or idiosyncratic phrase, ask what it meant, non-judgmentally and with real interest; the explanation often carries more substantial evidence of psychosis, and the patient guides you to the regions worth exploring." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Tapping an odd phrase", lines: [
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Tell me more about what the anxiety is like." },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(giggles) It's hard to say. I get wound up and I don't know what to do with myself. I think I'm just too nervous to be a daughter." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "Too nervous to be a daughter. What do you mean by that, exactly?", tag: "tapping the idiosyncratic phrase, with interest" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "The panic comes. Goose bumps everywhere. I never know when." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "And how does the panic connect to being a daughter?", tag: "looking for the logic" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "It just does. Daughters have things they're supposed to know and nobody tells you anymore. There's too much out there now. So I'm too nervous to be a daughter, and too nervous to be a mother either, so there it is." }
        ], note: "Composed. The phrase was odd, the patient did not notice it, the explanation was vague and mildly illogical: three soft signs in a row, none of them a hard sign, all of them reasons to look harder." },
        { type: "p", text: "Robinson's approach to delusional material is a gentle, genuine, respectful curiosity, because patients who harbor delusions are so immersed in them that an interested, conversational manner draws detailed information. Three aims: grease the wheels so the patient feels comfortable ('I'm interested in what you just said, tell me more'; 'how did this all start?'), uncover the extent and logic ('what has happened so far?'; 'why would someone want to do this to you?'), and determine how entrenched the delusion is ('how do you know this is the situation?'; 'how do you account for what has taken place?'). A screening entry is to ask about ideas the patient holds strongly that others around them do not share and may argue about. The patient's actions on the delusion measure distance and, in some delusions, danger: patients with delusions of infestation have mutilated themselves digging for parasites." },
        { type: "p", text: "Sooner or later a delusional patient asks whether you believe them. There is no single right answer, but there are good ones, and the way you handle the moment can decide the alliance, whether in a clinic, on a unit, or in the emergency department. Patients sharing a delusion for the first time fear being seen as seriously mentally ill (a fear that itself suggests some distance from the belief). Keep empathizing, and tactfully avoid becoming the arbiter of reality: 'I'm keeping an open mind'; 'I can't decide without more information'; 'my job is to understand your views'; or, Shea's favorite, 'the story is an unusual one, so I really want to hear more before deciding; tell me about...' and refer the patient back to an affectively charged detail, which both conveys respectful interest and moves the patient off the demand for a verdict. Never openly agree ('of course I believe you'): false endorsement undermines the alliance and returns later as 'then you'll call the police for me.' The only exception is the rare moment when the clinician judges that the patient may become violent without immediate agreement." },
        { type: "exchange", title: "Do you believe me?", lines: [
          { who: "Pt.", t: "Before I go on, I need to know. Do you believe me?" },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "It's an unusual story, and I want to make sure I understand it properly before I decide anything. Help me see exactly what the parasites are doing in your body right now.", tag: "open, non-affirming, back to a charged detail" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "(animated) They're in my head. I can't concentrate, I feel them shifting. And my feet burn when I walk." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "That sounds very disturbing.", tag: "actively empathizing" },
          { who: "Pt.", t: "It is. Nobody takes it seriously." },
          { who: "Clin.", t: "What have you been doing to try to get them out?", tag: "extent of action: distance and danger" }
        ], note: "Composed. The clinician neither endorses nor argues, keeps the patient talking, and ends on the question that will reveal whether the patient has been cutting at his skin." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c11p5", label: "Practice: tapping odd language" }, { kind: "practice", id: "c11p2", label: "Practice: responding to 'Do you believe me?'" }]
    },

    {
      id: "not-psychosis",
      title: "Hallucinations and beliefs that are not psychosis",
      lede: "Bereavement, sleep, failing eyes, culture and religion, and the test that separates a sanctioned experience from a psychotic one.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "A hard sign is not always psychosis, and mistaking a non-psychotic hallucination for one leads to disastrous labels. Studies put the lifetime prevalence of auditory hallucinations in people without psychiatric disorder anywhere from a few percent to 15–18%, with some estimates far higher. The commonest are [[Hypnagogic / hypnopompic hallucinations]], while falling asleep and waking. Extreme stress and prolonged sleep deprivation produce them. Bereavement does so often: in one study of 300 widowed men and women, nearly half had sensed their dead partner, visual slightly more than auditory, some for a decade or more, with no psychosis; older survivors were more likely to report it. In the elderly, remember [[Charles Bonnet syndrome]], in which central or ocular visual loss (cataracts, for instance) produces vivid, complex, purely visual hallucinations of people or small animals, a visual phantom limb reported by perhaps 60% of elderly patients with severe visual loss; one patient scattered birdseed for the birds walking about his room. It is easily mistaken for delirium or dementia, and correcting the eye defect ends it." },
        { type: "p", text: "Hallucinations are sometimes welcomed and sought. Socrates, Descartes, Caesar, Swedenborg, and Blake reported voices; oracles, priests, sadhus, and shamans have valued them, with and without drugs, for millennia. Beliefs in witchcraft, ghosts, channeling, spells, and extrasensory perception are ordinary in many cultures, including American New Age culture, and even a loosening of associations appears in sanctioned phenomena such as speaking in tongues. Nor does a disturbing hallucination prove psychosis, though disturbing ones are more likely psychotic. Shea's example: a Taiwanese woman after an abortion may hear a child persistently crying, understood in her culture as the child's dismay at a prevented reincarnation; disquieting, but not psychosis, and without that cultural knowledge she could be labeled with a brief psychotic disorder." },
        { type: "p", text: "How, then, do you decide when the same woman might in fact be developing schizophrenia, or the daughter of a Pentecostal minister (Chapter 6) whose beliefs about possession were shared by her mother and congregation is nonetheless psychotic? Ask whether the hallucination or belief is embedded in a [[Psychotic matrix]]: a cluster of delusional mood and delusional perception that was present before the phenomenon, accompanies it, and persists after it. Classic psychoses do not materialize out of nowhere and switch off; they have a prodrome and a residuum marked by soft signs. Look too for other hard signs not sanctioned by the culture; for a sanctioned belief twisted into a vicious, denigrating variant (Scott's Bantu example: customary guiding voices of known relatives become accusatory voices from unknown sources singling the hearer out); and for the two companions of true psychosis, an intensely disturbing quality and ongoing disruption of functioning, remembering that pleasant voices do not exclude schizophrenia (some patients miss them on medication)." },
        { type: "case", title: "The crying child, revisited", text: "Summarized from the chapter. Most post-abortion Taiwanese women who hear the child cry are not psychotic. The one who also describes weeks of feeling odd, a persistent sense of being watched, and a preoccupation with suspicious knocking from her heater may be: the sanctioned crying is embedded in an ongoing psychotic matrix, is being transformed by the process into something more intense, meaningful, and disruptive than her peers experience, and in time berating voices and delusions of demons may follow. Likewise the minister's daughter: her mother knew that what she saw was not what happened to congregants during possession or after exorcism." },
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Fay's outcome", text: "Summarized from the chapter. She never showed a hard sign in the interview, but vagueness, guardedness, inappropriate giggles, bits of illogical thought, a preoccupation with a childhood event involving her brother, fleeting paranoia and magical thinking she attributed to 'a bad family,' moody periods and angry outbursts, and evasiveness about past suicide gestures left the clinician certain something was lurking. He wrote generalized anxiety disorder (provisional), rule out unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder, rule out schizotypal and borderline personality, and arranged rapid, close follow-up. Weeks later she was admitted with striking paranoid delusions about her brother and many symptoms of mania; he had seen her before the storm, in delusional mood or perception, or perhaps already holding delusions she was not ready to share. Later history of mixed affective instability with early psychosis moved the diagnosis to schizoaffective disorder, rule out bipolar disorder; she stabilized on lithium and haloperidol, and lifelong manipulativeness, drama, anger, and impulsivity kept a personality disorder in the differential (Chapter 14). A patient can have both." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch11", label: "Quiz: Chapter 11" }]
    },

    {
      id: "delirium",
      title: "Mr. Lawrence: the delirium hiding inside a known illness",
      lede: "The fingerprint rule, what delirium is, the two tip-offs, four bedside tests, and the causes.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Mr. Lawrence", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A man of about 30 with long-standing paranoid schizophrenia and past violence was found smashing a typewriter through his window and flinging paint over his furniture, screaming. Committed involuntarily, he tried to beat a nurse in the emergency department and was restrained and given haloperidol; the request came to admit him by phone directly to seclusion for another relapse. Shea, on call, insisted on seeing him. By then he lay calm and cooperative, denied hard signs of psychosis (though he mentioned suicidal thoughts days earlier), and the restraints came off. Walking to the toilet he staggered, looked around the bathroom as if teleported there, asked what he was doing in it, and denied having asked to go. He grew drowsy, blamed a night of drinking, could repeat only three or four digits forward where he had managed seven earlier, and began grasping at invisible objects near his feet. The interview stopped; Shea voiced his suspicion of an overdose despite the denials; the ECG showed subtle abnormalities; about thirty minutes later he stopped breathing and was saved on a ventilator. The diagnosis: a tricyclic overdose causing a paradoxical rage and then a delirium. Imagine the outcome had he gone to the seclusion room." },
        { type: "p", text: "The trap was assuming the old cause. Everyone, crisis clinician and emergency staff alike, read the rage as his schizophrenia because he had always presented paranoid and violent. Shea's principle: a psychosis of a single etiology tends to present similarly in each episode for a given person, soft signs and hard signs alike, an identifying fingerprint. When a new episode looks distinctly different from the old ones (Mr. Lawrence usually had delusions and hallucinations; this time he had neither), suspect that something new has been added. The electronic record of past episodes is invaluable here. And any patient presenting with delirium requires immediate medical evaluation, a diagnosis frequently missed, physicians included." },
        { type: "p", text: "[[Delirium]] in the DSM-5 is a disturbance of attention (the ability to direct, focus, sustain, and shift it) and awareness (orientation to the environment) that develops over hours to days, is a change from baseline, fluctuates through the day, is accompanied by another cognitive disturbance (memory, orientation, language, visuospatial ability, perception), is not explained by an established or evolving neurocognitive disorder or by coma, and has evidence of a physiological cause: a medical condition, intoxication or withdrawal, a toxin, or several together. Not every delirious patient is disoriented, especially early, which is one reason the diagnosis is missed. Practically, a diffuse pathophysiological dysfunction of the brain shows itself in one of two ways, a fluctuating level of consciousness or a marked problem concentrating and attending to the environment, and if either appears in a patient with soft or hard signs of psychosis, think delirium and work it up." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Presentation", "What you see", "Why it is missed"], rows: [
          ["Hypoactive ('quiet delirium'), the commonest", "Drowsy, hard to arouse", "Bothers no one, especially in the elderly; overlooked"],
          ["Hyperactive", "'Wired,' over-responsive to stimulation, driven, often agitated or aggressive (Mr. Williams)", "Read as a psychiatric relapse or 'behavior'"],
          ["Mixed", "Swings between the two, sometimes within hours (Mr. Lawrence: rage, then drowsiness)", "The doctor on rounds sees the good hour; the night nurse saw the delirium; brain damage can accrue between"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The EEG parallels the level of consciousness (diffuse slowing in hypoactive states, fast activity in hyperactive ones, sometimes normal). Subtle attentional problems are harder to spot than fluctuating consciousness and may not be apparent unless tested; sometimes natural, intelligent conversation is reassurance enough, but when you are suspicious, test formally. Four bedside tests as a battery catch most significant deficits." },
        { type: "steps", items: [
          "[[Digit span]] forward and backward: explain that you will give numbers to remember, starting with two and lengthening; the patient waits until you point before repeating, so immediate recall rather than echo is tested. Say the digits in a steady rhythm so they cannot be chunked like a phone number. Average adults manage about seven forward and four to five backward.",
          "[[Vigilance test]]: recite random letters for one to two minutes; the patient taps the table at every 'A.' Errors of omission and commission accumulate as the string lengthens, and some delirious patients forget which letter they were hunting. Shea finds it surprisingly sensitive for subtle delirium.",
          "Constructions: copying a cross or a cube. Note time and ease as well as accuracy; an engineer who labors over a cube is telling you something.",
          "Handwriting: name, a sentence of their own, a copied sentence. Dysgraphia shows as spelling errors, clumsy letters, reduplicated strokes in M or N, misalignment; far more telling with a prior sample for comparison, and limited to literate patients (printing errors count too).",
          "With a hostile patient who will not play word games, observe instead: inability to follow commands, forgetting the question, over-sensitivity to noise, or plain confusion, as in the bathroom. Chapter 16 and Video Module 16.2 demonstrate the four tests."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Inouye's [[Confusion Assessment Method]] brings a structured approach in about five minutes, focusing on acuteness of onset, inattention, disturbed thinking, altered consciousness, disorientation, memory impairment, perceptual disturbance, psychomotor agitation, and psychomotor retardation; a manual is available online. Other common features: hallucinations or illusions in 40 to 75% (visual ones should always raise suspicion of an organic state); rapidly changing, malleable delusions, often paranoid, unlike the stable delusions of schizophrenia or delusional disorder; derailment or illogical thought; impaired short-term memory and orientation; fluctuation with worse confusion at night ([[Sundowning]]); onset over hours or days, though insidious onsets occur; a possible contribution from stress and psychological mechanisms; abnormal affect, often fear and anxiety; misidentifying the unfamiliar as familiar (an aide becomes a sibling); and odd behaviors such as wandering, drinking from the toilet, 'occupational delirium' (work behaviors performed in the hospital bed), and carphology, picking at the bedclothes." },
        { type: "table", head: ["Category", "Common causes of delirium"], rows: [
          ["Metabolic", "Hypoxia, hypercarbia, anemia; electrolyte imbalance and hyperosmolarity; hyper- or hypoglycemia; abnormal magnesium or calcium; end-stage liver or kidney disease; thiamine deficiency (Wernicke's encephalopathy); thyroid, parathyroid, and adrenal disorders"],
          ["Infection", "Systemic (pneumonia, sepsis, malaria, typhoid); intracranial (meningitis, encephalitis)"],
          ["Neurological", "Hypertensive crisis, stroke, subarachnoid hemorrhage, vasculitis; seizures; trauma"],
          ["Withdrawal", "Alcohol (hallucinosis, withdrawal seizures, DTs); barbiturates and other sedatives"],
          ["Intoxication", "Digoxin, levodopa, anticholinergics, street drugs"],
          ["Post-operative", "Especially after cardiac surgery"]
        ] },
        { type: "pearl", title: "Make it a habit", text: "Rule out delirium every time a patient presents with psychosis. Unless viewing delirium as part of the differential becomes automatic, you will miss it, and the patient pays with permanent brain damage or worse. Mr. Lawrence's formulation: medication-induced delirium (antidepressant overdose), schizophrenia in remission, respiratory arrest." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c11p4", label: "Practice: checking attention in a known patient" }]
    },

    {
      id: "medical",
      title: "Kate: medical psychosis without delirium, and the screening physical",
      lede: "A 14-year-old who was called hysterical twice, the life-threatening causes, and the five-part examination.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Kate", text: "Summarized from the chapter. Her parents' faces said something was very wrong. Kate, 14, slightly overweight, had grown more depressed over two months; five days earlier nobody came to her slumber party, and since then she had been odd, talking about 'reality' and wandering the house. Two nights before, she knocked on her parents' door at 2 a.m., topless, saying in a monotone that she needed to talk. Never delusional, never hearing voices. Two emergency departments had called her hysterical and referred her for outpatient therapy. In the interview she seemed pulled into a cocoon, looked Shea in the eye and asked him what reality was, giggled inappropriately, showed halting speech with mild thought blocking and distractibility, and during a break wandered the exam area peeking behind other patients' curtains. She was fully oriented, alert, and stable, with only trivial errors on reversed digits and the vigilance test. Her physical examination was normal, without neck stiffness or headache. The soft signs earned admission; Shea, unable to explain the picture, ordered an immediate neurological consultation on arrival. Blood work and a lumbar puncture revealed viral encephalitis. A week after admission she lay dying in intensive care." },
        { type: "p", text: "Kate was not delirious. Not every medical cause of psychosis produces a delirium; encephalitis and the rest of the long organic list (space-occupying lesions, hypoxia, neurological and vascular disease, infection, metabolic and endocrine disorders, nutritional deficiencies, drugs and toxins) can present exactly like schizophrenia or bipolar disorder. Her formulation at admission, before any results: unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder (provisional), with rule-outs for brief psychotic disorder, psychotic disorder due to another medical condition, substance-induced disorders, major depression with psychotic features, and delirium; medically, rule out infection and complex partial seizures. The thought blocking was a sign of active psychotic process; the topless visit and the wandering were the odd behaviors that belong in the differential of psychosis. Think organic, every time." },
        { type: "list", items: [
          "The common life-threatening causes of acute psychosis: hypoglycemia; hypertensive encephalopathy; poor oxygenation (myocardial infarction, pulmonary embolus, anemia, hemorrhage); encephalitis or meningitis; drugs, including medications, street drugs, withdrawal, industrial toxins, and poisons; intracranial trauma, hemorrhage, or raised pressure; Wernicke's encephalopathy (a medical emergency because untreated it leaves permanent damage).",
          "Also serious: hepatic failure, uremia, subacute bacterial endocarditis, chronic subdural hematoma, and autoimmune encephalitis, which can present with psychosis, catatonia, eating-disorder symptoms, obsessions, anxiety, depression, and lethargy, usually with memory and cognitive problems and often followed by seizures, ataxia, parkinsonism, or memory loss.",
          "The list is short and the entities easy to rule out if you remember them; because they are rare, they are overlooked unless you train yourself to consider them every time."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "A brief, directed physical examination uncovers many of these, though not all (Kate's was normal). A patient with new psychotic symptoms should seldom if ever leave an emergency department without one, and a clinic or counseling center should get the patient examined as soon as possible after the assessment. Five areas: vital signs (retake abnormal ones; agitation alone seldom sustains a pulse above 120 to 130); autonomic dysfunction, including the [[Anticholinergic syndrome]] remembered as hot as a pepper, blind as a bat, dry as a bone, red as a beet, mad as a hatter, which is often incomplete or masked (Mr. Lawrence, on an anticholinergic antidepressant, had a fast pulse and dry mouth but normal reactive pupils and pale skin); heart and lungs when indicated; neurological signs and head trauma (focal weakness, reflexes including primitive reflexes and the Babinski sign, neck rigidity, blood behind the eardrum or other signs of skull fracture); and the eyes (pupil size, reactivity, and asymmetry, nystagmus, fundi for raised pressure)." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "The seclusion-room error", text: "A common mistake is to admit a wildly agitated patient straight to seclusion and never return for the examination once they have calmed. Once the patient is calm, the physician, nurse clinician, or physician assistant should attempt the screening examination no matter the hour; if the suspicion of serious illness is strong, restraint to allow examination may be justified." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "practice", id: "c11p4", label: "Practice: checking attention in a known patient" }]
    },

    {
      id: "micropsychotic",
      title: "Ms. Flagstone: micropsychotic episodes, flashbacks, and the great masquerader",
      lede: "Psychosis that lasts minutes, the personality disorders that produce it, PTSD, and partial complex seizures.",
      blocks: [
        { type: "case", title: "Ms. Flagstone", text: "Summarized from the chapter. A stylish woman arrives at a community mental health center waving a cigarette until the receptionist asks her to put it out. She describes a life 'not going anywhere' and poor relationships with men, with shifting affect, brief tears quickly mastered, normal speech, mild tangentiality, no loosening or blocking, full orientation, and no delusions. She has heard a voice once, two years ago, and has told no one: at a terrible time in her life, washing dishes, a light filled the kitchen, she knew it was God calling her back, and from the light the angel Gabriel spoke to her, announcing a child, testing her, conversing with God's own voice, and finally telling her she was back with her Father. It lasted about fifteen minutes (perhaps up to thirty), the voice was loud and distinct, there were no soft signs before or after, and she claims no mission beyond being a good Christian. Near that time she had been fired and felt slightly paranoid around co-workers. She also has ten-minute spells under stress of feeling unreal, not quite herself. On further interviewing she seems to meet many criteria for histrionic personality disorder, though that cannot be settled in an hour." },
        { type: "p", text: "She has never had ongoing psychotic process, so what explains the voice and the depersonalization? Psychosis is not confined to the major diagnoses. Several personality disorders produce [[Micropsychotic episode|micropsychotic episodes]] lasting minutes to hours (once episodes last a day or more, suspect a more serious psychotic disorder), precipitated by stress, drug use, or both, and typically consisting of fleeting paranoid ideation, depersonalization, and derealization; frequent stress or drugs increase both frequency and duration. They are seen most in paranoid, schizotypal, and borderline personality disorders, and rarely in histrionic or narcissistic ones when defenses are overwhelmed (Shea's example is a respected, severely narcissistic priest exposed as a pedophile, whose humiliation could produce intermittent lapses into paranoia)." },
        { type: "compare",
          a: { title: "Classic psychotic disorders", items: [
            "Hard signs embedded in a psychotic matrix",
            "A prodrome of days, weeks, or months with soft signs before hard signs",
            "A residuum after the episode",
            "The phenomenology that separates them from culturally sanctioned experiences"
          ] },
          b: { title: "Abrupt psychotic process", items: [
            "Micropsychotic episodes: no prodrome of soft signs, abrupt onset, abrupt end with immediate return to baseline",
            "Street drugs and medications: hallucinations within minutes to hours (LSD, peyote, mescaline)",
            "Seizure disorders and other neurological processes",
            "Intense PTSD flashbacks reaching psychotic proportions"
          ] }
        },
        { type: "p", text: "Flashbacks deserve a word. People with intense flashbacks commonly hear the voices and sounds of the original trauma. In an [[Atypical flashback]] the emotions of the assault (extreme fear, anxiety) return without any memory of the event, and such episodes are easily misread as enduring psychosis or paranoia. Shea's patient would, without warning, arm herself and point a gun at her front door for an hour or two, with no images, only fear and hypervigilance; the trigger was a ringing telephone, and it was conjectured that a phone had rung unanswered during a repressed childhood assault." },
        { type: "p", text: "Ms. Flagstone's voices and depersonalization probably merit the label of micropsychotic episodes: stress-triggered, abrupt, and without the mood states that would suggest soft signs. Her formulation after one interview: no clear general psychiatric disorder (cyclothymia or dysthymia kept in mind for the tangentiality and mood shifts); histrionic personality disorder, provisional, rule out a mixed personality disorder with schizotypal and borderline traits; and, medically, rule out partial complex seizures. That last line surprises until you hear the rest: she had become intensely interested in philosophy and religion, filled nearly twenty journals with non-psychotic writing, and reported brief uncomfortable abdominal sensations rising toward her throat." },
        { type: "p", text: "Epilepsy with [[Partial complex seizures]] (formerly temporal lobe epilepsy) is the masquerader par excellence; it can mimic almost any psychiatric disturbance and is especially good at mimicking psychosis, so a query belongs in every psychotic presentation (Kate's included, given her distracted wandering). Symptoms can emerge during the seizure or between seizures. The aura may bring fear, anxiety, déjà vu or jamais vu, strange pungent odors, and peculiar abdominal feelings, which can be the only symptom ('abdominal seizures'); as the seizure develops the patient loses awareness and shows automatisms, picking, wandering, bizarre mannerisms. Three questions uncover it: have you ever found yourself somewhere without knowing how you got there; have you had periods of losing consciousness; have friends or family seen you doing very odd things you do not remember. Between seizures, [[Interictal personality change]] can appear: preoccupation with religious and moral questions, copious writing, reduced sexual drive, intense mystical experiences, deepened and intensified emotion, and interpersonal viscosity, the wish to keep talking and stay near people. Family and friends, puzzled by episodes of confusion or aimless behavior the patient cannot recall, are especially useful collateral. Labeling such a patient schizophrenic robs them of anticonvulsants and exposes them to antipsychotics for nothing." },
        { type: "pearl", title: "The person beneath the disease", text: "Shea closes the chapter's survey with Osler: it matters more to know what sort of patient has a disease than what sort of disease a patient has. This chapter taught the disease; Chapter 12 teaches the person who lives inside it." }
      ],
      practice: [{ kind: "quiz", id: "ch11", label: "Quiz: Chapter 11" }]
    }
  ],

  deeper: [
    { title: "Read in the book", text: "The DSM-5 criteria boxes reprinted in full (schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, delirium) with their specifiers; de Nerval's own pages from the asylum garden; the patients' first-person accounts of delusional mood and delusional perception; the complete police interview; Robinson's illustrative dialogue and his list of responses to 'Do you believe me?'; Resnick's reconstructed interview; the digit-span instructions; Tables 11.1 (causes of delirium) and 11.2 (organic causes of psychosis); and Figures 11.1 to 11.3 on the life cycle of a psychosis and the diversity of etiologies." },
    { title: "Video modules and related chapters", text: "Video Module 16.2 demonstrates the four bedside tests of attention (digit span, vigilance, constructions, handwriting) in the mental status examination; Chapter 16 defines the affect and thought-process terms cited here. Chapter 6 for the kulturbrille and the minister's daughter; Chapter 9 for mood-congruent and mood-incongruent psychosis, dysphoric mania, and the bipolar trap; Chapter 12 for the person beneath the psychosis and the exploration of hallucinations; Chapter 14 for the personality disorders that produce micropsychotic episodes; Chapter 18 for violence assessment." },
    { title: "Sources Shea draws on", text: "Fish on the quality of hallucinations; Goldfrank on PCP and Fischer and Harrison on substance use; Rado on the deficit syndrome; Guze on affective syndromes in schizophrenia; Tsuang, and later Bruijnzeel and Tandon, on the evolving concept of schizophrenia and the psychosis spectrum; Lopez-Ibor and Kurt Schneider on delusional mood and perception; Robinson's writing on eliciting delusions; Resnick on confrontation with a paranoid persecutor; Castle on partition delusions in paraphrenia; Inouye's Confusion Assessment Method; Bassuk and Beck's Emergency Psychiatry for the organic differential; Scott on hallucinations among the Bantu; and Bear and colleagues on interictal change in temporal lobe epilepsy." }
  ]
};
